# Line endings. This file exists for one reason: Git for Windows defaults to
# core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout. A shell script that
# arrives with CRLF fails on the FIRST LINE with
#
#     bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M: no such file or directory
#
# — an error that names the interpreter, so it reads as "bash is broken" rather
# than "the file was mangled on the way in". Nothing in the tool can detect or
# recover from it, because nothing in the tool gets to run.
#
# ⛔ Do not remove these lines to "clean up". They are the whole defence, and the
#    failure they prevent is invisible until a Windows machine tries to install.

# Baseline: let git normalise text in the repository, decide per-file below.
* text=auto

# ── Executed files — MUST be LF on every platform ─────────────────────────────
# These are run, not just read. A CRLF in them is a syntax error, not a cosmetic
# difference.
*.sh              text eol=lf
*.py              text eol=lf
*.bash            text eol=lf

# The git hook that refuses a commit carrying the person's own material. It has
# no extension, so the *.sh rule above does not reach it — and it is the single
# worst file to lose to a line-ending bug, because when it fails to run there is
# no error, just an unprotected repository.
system/githooks/*  text eol=lf

# system/pulse-config.md is CODE by another name: system/tools/pulse.sh and
# install-schedulers.sh both parse its ```jobs```/```crontab``` fences with exact-string
# `[ "$line" = '```jobs' ]` bash comparisons, not a tolerant parser — a CRLF from the *.md
# rule below (issue #75) breaks that match silently: both scripts find zero rows and BOTH
# report success, so the entire scheduled-automation layer goes silently absent on a Windows
# install. Same class of failure as the shell scripts above; same fix, scoped to this one
# file rather than every *.md — see the note below on why this stays narrow.
system/pulse-config.md  text eol=lf

# ── Data and documents — normalise, but harmless either way ───────────────────
# NOT widened to `*.md text eol=lf` project-wide: pulse-config.md is the one markdown file
# in this repo that is byte-exact parsed by a shell script (see above); the other ~300 are
# prose or are read by Python parsers that already normalize \r via `.strip()`/universal-
# newlines, so a blanket pin would carry the same cost pattern this file's own header warns
# against — a rule that "fixes" files that were never broken. Pin the next one individually
# if the same failure shape (exact-string fence match, from a shell script) shows up again.
*.md              text
*.json            text
*.yaml            text
*.yml             text
*.txt             text

# ── Never touch ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.zip             binary
*.png             binary
*.jpg             binary
*.gif             binary
*.pdf             binary
